{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgb9R1LR17ZtLzQeDZDXM","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01VJpaKbEs68ynJMzo76jmfM","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cat docs/specs/task-edit.spec.md docs/specs/task-dependencies.spec.md; head -c 3000 .argus-delegate/*.out; git status --short | head"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2359,"cache_read_input_tokens":26032,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2359},"output_tokens":16,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"e60805ed-c36d-4e22-8274-acc0a44d64d1","uuid":"a2ec5043-6a29-4b1b-bce5-c95e3191fc61","timestamp":"2026-10-31T23:00:27.948Z","request_id":"req_011Cgb9R13oEPvzP6WGHSday","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01VJpaKbEs68ynJMzo76jmfM":{"command":"cd /workspace/agent/task-workspaces/030540ef-d69a-4c2c-839d-f4970e8c94d7 && cat docs/specs/task-edit.spec.md docs/specs/task-dependencies.spec.md; head -c 3000 .argus-delegate/*.out; git status --short | head"}},"wire_ingest_context":{"toolu_01VJpaKbEs68ynJMzo76jmfM":{"cwd":"/workspace/agent/task-workspaces/030540ef-d69a-4c2c-839d-f4970e8c94d7"}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01VJpaKbEs68ynJMzo76jmfM","type":"tool_result","content":"# Task edit\n\n# 1. Purpose and Scope\n\nAn operator or orchestrator who spots a wrong title, a thin spec, a missing\nacceptance criterion or a forgotten prerequisite can only cancel and recreate\nthe task today (only dependencies are editable, [task\ndependencies](task-dependencies.spec.md) 3.1). This spec adds **editing an\nexisting task's definition** with the same behaviour on every surface:\n\n- REST `PATCH /api/tasks/{id}`;\n- MCP `edit_task`;\n- the dashboard's Edit action on the task detail.\n\nAll three call one function, `tasks.service.edit_task`, which owns every rule\nbelow. `PUT /api/tasks/{id}/dependencies` and `set_task_dependencies` stay, as\nthin callers of the same function with only `depends_on` set.\n\n# 2. Non-Goals\n\n- No change to creation, retry, cancel, approval, archive or PR management.\n- No editing of a running attempt: an edit never reaches a worker that already\n  holds the task (3.4).\n- No edit history browser in the UI beyond the existing History list; the audit\n  is the `task.edited` event (3.7).\n- No per-project permissions (none exist, [knowledge](knowledge.spec.md) 3.1).\n\n# 3. Decisions Made\n\n## 3.1 Fields\n\n**Editable** (the only keys `changes` accepts):\n\n| Field | Type | Rules (same as `TaskCreate`) |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `title` | string | 1–200 characters; cannot be cleared |\n| `spec` | string | any length; `null` or `\"\"` clears it to `\"\"` |\n| `acceptance` | list of strings | `null` or `[]` clears |\n| `priority` | integer | cannot be cleared (send `0` to reset) |\n| `required_capabilities` | list of strings | de-duplicated and sorted, as at creation; `null` or `[]` clears |\n| `knowledge` | list of page ids | replaces the pinned set; every page must exist and belong to the task's project or to `global` (`422 knowledge: …`); `null` or `[]` unpins all. Pins a *kind* adds at creation are not re-added |\n| `difficulty` | `trivial` \\| `simple` \\| `standard` \\| `hard` | cannot be cleared; sets `difficulty_source` to `declared` ([task difficulty](task-difficulty.spec.md)) |\n| `depends_on` | list of task ids | replaces the set; the rules of [task dependencies](task-dependencies.spec.md) 3.1: ids exist, no self-dependency, no cycle, at most 100 |\n| `account_providers` | list of providers | engine providers only, de-duplicated and sorted, as at creation ([local harness](local-harness.spec.md) 3); `null` or `[]` clears (no restriction) |\n| `harness_model`, `executor_model` | object | [task models](task-models.spec.md) 3.1; `null` clears |\n\n**Immutable**: `state`/`status`, `assignee_agent_id`, `lease_expires_at`,\n`lease_ttl_s`, `timeout_s`, `timeout_at`, `attempt`, `failures`,\n`max_attempts`, `result`, `error`, `block_reason`, `progress_*`, `input`,\n`review`, `requires_approval`, `created_*`, `updated_at`, `archived_at`, any\nevent or history, and the structural ones `project`, `parent_id`, `kind`\n(and `id`). Naming any of them (or any unknown key) in `changes` is\n`422 immutable_field`, nothing is written, and `details` lists the offending\nkeys. They are not options of the MCP tool or of the dashboard form; the\nschema documents only the editable fields. `block_reason` is read-only in the\ntask detail facts too: it is shown as the inbox blocked card's sentence (worker\nlifecycle 3.2) and never edited there. State changes go through their own\noperations (approve, cancel, retry, …).\n\n## 3.2 Patch semantics\n\n- `changes` is a partial patch: a key that is **omitted is preserved**.\n  An empty `changes` is `422 validation_failed` (`changes: nothing to change`).\n- `null` is the explicit **clear** for `spec`, `acceptance`,\n  `required_capabilities`, `knowledge` and `depends_on` (equivalent to `\"\"` or\n  `[]`). `null` for `title` or `priority` is `422`.\n- A list is replaced as a whole, never merged.\n- A patch whose values all equal the current ones is a no-op: nothing is\n  written, no event, the version does not change, the current task comes back.\n- Validation is **atomic**: the patch is checked as a whole inside the write\n  transaction, before the first write. One bad field rejects the edit and the\n  task is untouched.\n\n## 3.3 Version precondition\n\nThe request carries `expected_version`, the `version` the caller read\n([MCP operations](mcp-operations.spec.md) 3.1). A mismatch is\n`409 stale_version` with the current version in `details`; nothing changes. A\nsuccessful edit that changed something always yields a new, strictly greater\n`version`, even within the clock's resolution. The response is the task, with\nits new `version`, and `get_task`/`get_tasks` read back the same version and\nvalues immediately.\n\n## 3.4 What each state permits\n\nThe single source of truth is `argus_core.task_edit.EDITABLE_BY_STATE`. The API\nreturns the allowed set as `editable_fields` on every task; the dashboard form\nand the MCP tool description are driven by it.\n\n| State | Editable fields | Why |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `awaiting_approval`, `pending`, `ready` | all seven | nothing has run; the next claim sees the saved definition |\n| `blocked`, `claimed`, `in_review` | `priority` | queue order only; the title, spec, criteria, capabilities, pins or dependencies may already be in use by an attempt or a reviewer |\n| `succeeded`, `failed`, `cancelled` | none | the record of what ran stays as it was |\n| archived (any) | none | archived tasks are read-only |\n\nA change outside the state's set is `409 task_not_editable`; `details` lists\n`state: <state>`, `not editable: <fields>` and `editable: <fields>`. We block;\nthere is no \"edit now, acknowledge later\" path.\n\n**Edit versus claim.** Writes are serialized, so the two outcomes are the only\nones: an edit that commits first is seen by the claim (the worker gets the new\ndefinition); a claim that commits first changes the task's `version` and state,\nso the edit fails `409 stale_version` (or `task_not_editable` if it was\nprepared against the new version). An edit never changes a task after a worker\nholds it, and Argus never assumes it updates a running attempt's prompt: a\nclaimed task keeps working on the definition it was handed.\n\n## 3.5 Attempt snapshot\n\nAt every claim Argus stores the definition the worker is given in\n`task_attempts` (`task_id`, `attempt`, `agent_id`, `claimed_at`, `definition`:\nthe seven editable fields plus `kind`, `project`). The definition is never\nupdated and rows are never deleted; an operator release only sets the row's\n`outcome` (`released`) and `outcome_reason` (migration 0031,\n[worker lifecycle](worker-lifecycle.spec.md) 3.3); `attempt` restarts at 1 after a retry, so they are listed in claim order. `GET /api/tasks/{id}/attempts` lists them, oldest first, so the\ndefinition of any attempt can be told apart from a later edit.\n\n## 3.6 Dependent state\n\nAfter `depends_on` changes the task follows the existing logic in the same\ntransaction ([task dependencies](task-dependencies.spec.md) 3.1): a `ready`\ntask with an unfinished dependency goes to `pending`; a `pending` one whose\ndependencies all `succeeded` becomes `ready`; `awaiting_approval` keeps its\nstate. `task.dependencies_changed` and `task.dependency_failed` events are\nraised as before.\n\n## 3.7 Audit\n\nEvery edit that changes something records one `task.edited` event in the\ntransaction: actor (`admin:<label>` or `agent:<id>`), time, `from_version`,\n`to_version` and `changes`, a diff per changed field:\n\n- `title`, `priority`, `complexity`: `{from, to}`;\n- `required_capabilities`, `knowledge`, `depends_on`: `{added, removed}` ids;\n- `spec`, `acceptance`: only lengths/counts and a SHA-256 digest of the old and\n  new text. The text itself is never copied into the event log, which is\n  streamed and shown in History: whatever an operator pasted into a spec, a\n  secret included, does not spread. The text of what each attempt ran is in the\n  attempt snapshot (3.5).\n\n## 3.8 Permissions\n\nOrchestrator and admin tokens edit; worker tokens get `403 forbidden` on REST\nand on MCP (the tool is not listed to them). This is the same authorization as\n`set_task_dependencies`; nothing is widened. There are no per-project\npermissions: project and kind cannot change, a knowledge page of another\nproject cannot be pinned, and error messages never echo another project's page\ncontent. `403` is decided before the version, state or content is looked at.\n\n## 3.9 Errors\n\nDomain errors keep the shape `{detail: {code, message, details}}` on REST and\n`<status> <code>: <message> | <details>` as a tool error on MCP.\n\n| Status | `code` | When |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| 403 | `forbidden` | not an orchestrator/admin token |\n| 404 | `not_found` | task, dependency or pinned page does not exist |\n| 409 | `stale_version` | `expected_version` is not current; `details`: `[current version]` |\n| 409 | `task_not_editable` | a field is outside the state's set (3.4) |\n| 422 | `immutable_field` | an immutable or unknown key (3.1) |\n| 422 | `validation_failed` | a value breaks a rule; each `details` entry is `<field>: <why>` |\n| 422 | `dependency_cycle` | self or transitive dependency; `details`: the path |\n\n## 3.10 Dashboard\n\nThe task detail shows **Edit** when `editable_fields` is not empty. The form is\nprefilled from the task, fields outside `editable_fields` are disabled, and\nSave sends only the fields the operator changed with the task's `version`.\nPer-field errors (`details` entries `<field>: …`, `dependency_cycle` and\nmissing dependencies on *Depends on*) show beside their field. On\n`stale_version` the form stays open with the operator's draft untouched, says\nwho is ahead, and offers *Reload latest* (discard the draft) or *Keep editing\non the latest version* (re-base the draft on the current version). A re-based\ndraft that changes a field the new state does not allow shows that field's\nerror instead of dropping the change. Live\nrefreshes never overwrite a draft. Cancel closes without a request.\n\n# 4. Open Questions\n\n- Whether `claimed` tasks should accept a spec edit delivered to the running\n  worker (a clarification-like message). Not without a worker-side contract;\n  [task steering](task-steering.spec.md) proposes one, as a message rather than an\n  edit.\n- Whether kind pins should be re-merged on edit.\n\n# 5. Constraints\n\n- One write transaction per edit, `tx.now` for time, no network call inside it.\n- One service function for all surfaces; REST, MCP and UI hold no rules of their\n  own and the MCP tool forwards to REST like every other tool.\n- The only schema change is the additive `task_attempts` table (migration 0024).\n- No secrets in the audit (3.7).\n\n# 6. Implementation Consequences\n\nOne PR:\n\n1. This spec, its index link, and pointers in task dependencies,\n   MCP operations and worker lifecycle (3.6, 3.7).\n2. Migration `0024_task_attempts.sql`; `argus_core.task_edit` (fields, state\n   policy, patch model); `Task.version` and `Task.editable_fields`.\n3. `tasks.service.edit_task` (and `set_dependencies` delegating to it), the\n   claim snapshot, `PATCH /api/tasks/{id}`, `GET /api/tasks/{id}/attempts`.\n4. MCP `edit_task`, instructions and tool catalog; OpenAPI and the TypeScript\n   client regenerated.\n5. Dashboard form, helpers and tests.\n6. Tests: parity (REST, MCP over the protocol, readback), atomic validation,\n   dependent state, concurrency and the edit/claim race, per-state policy,\n   permissions and immutables on both surfaces, snapshots, audit, and\n   dashboard component tests. Browser screenshots under `docs/evidence/` need\n   Playwright, which this repo does not have; they are not part of this PR.\n# Task dependencies\n\n# 1. Purpose and Scope\n\nDependencies are the only control flow between tasks\n([worker lifecycle](worker-lifecycle.spec.md) 3.2): a task with unfinished\ndependencies waits in `pending` and becomes `ready` once all of them\n`succeeded`. This spec closes three gaps in that model:\n\n1. **Editing.** Dependencies are fixed at creation. An orchestrator cannot add a\n   prerequisite it forgot or drop one that no longer matters, although the\n   scope table (worker lifecycle 3.6) says orchestrators \"edit deps\".\n2. **Visibility.** A dependent of a `failed` or `cancelled` task stays `pending`\n   with only a `task.dependency_failed` event. The inbox does not show it, so\n   it waits unnoticed.\n3. **Stranding.** A `cancelled` dependency can never succeed and cannot be\n   removed, so its dependents can only be cancelled or recreated.\n\n# 2. Non-Goals\n\n- No cascading: Argus still never cancels or fails a dependent on its own.\n- No ordering by shared resource (one task at a time per repository, say).\n  Order between tasks is declared, never inferred. That is a separate spec.\n- No conditional dependencies (\"run if A failed\"). A dependency means\n  \"succeeded first\".\n- No editing once a task was claimed: from `claimed` on, its work may already\n  rely on what its dependencies produced.\n\n# 3. Decisions Made\n\n## 3.1 Replacing dependencies\n\nSince the [task edit](task-edit.spec.md) spec this endpoint and tool call the same\nfunction as `PATCH /api/tasks/{id}` with only `depends_on`; the rules below hold\nunchanged, including the `task_not_waiting` code.\n\n- `PUT /api/tasks/{id}/dependencies` with `{depends_on: [ids], expected_version}`\n  (orchestrator or admin) → MCP `set_task_dependencies`. It replaces the whole\n  set; the response is the task, and its `version` changes.\n- Allowed while the task is `pending`, `awaiting_approval` or `ready`; otherwise\n  `409 task_not_waiting`. `expected_version` is the task's `version` (its\n  `updated_at`, MCP operations 3.1); a mismatch is `409 stale_version` with the\n  current version in `details`.\n- Every id must exist (`404`), the task cannot depend on itself, and the new set\n  cannot close a cycle: if a new dependency already depends on the task,\n  directly or transitively, it is `422 dependency_cycle` with the path in\n  `details`. At most 100 dependencies.\n- The state follows the new set in the same transaction: a `ready` task with an\n  unfinished dependency goes back to `pending` (a new `deps_added` action, the\n  only way back from `ready`; nothing holds a lease in `ready`); a `pending` task\n  whose dependencies all `succeeded` becomes `ready` (`deps_satisfied`, as\n  today). `awaiting_approval` keeps its state; approval already checks the\n  dependencies.\n- It records `task.dependencies_changed` with `added` and `removed`. A newly\n  added dependency that is already `failed` or `cancelled` raises\n  `task.dependency_failed`, as at creation.\n- Removing a `cancelled` or `failed` dependency is how a stranded task is\n  released (gap 3): no other mechanism is added.\n- A read-only [prerequisite evidence](task-discovery.spec.md) view reports\n  whether each direct dependency is forge-merged or artifact-verified. It\n  changes nothing here: readiness still keys only on `succeeded`.\n\n## 3.2 Stuck dependents in the inbox\n\n- The inbox (MCP operations 3.2) also lists `pending` tasks with at least one\n  `failed` or `cancelled` dependency. Their row has no decision and carries\n  `failed_dependencies: [ids]`; every other row has an empty list. They wait\n  since the latest of those dependencies ended.\n- `states` accepts `pending` for these rows; they count under `pending` in\n  `totals_by_state` and under `none` in `totals_by_decision_kind`.\n- The orchestrator then retries the dependency (`retry_task`, if `failed`),\n  replaces the dependencies (3.1), or cancels the dependent.\n\n# 4. Open Questions\n\n- Whether a dependent's claim should carry its dependencies' result summaries,\n  so a worker does not have to read them with `get_tasks`. Decide once workers\n  ask for it.\n- Whether readiness should require merged/verified delivery evidence rather\n  than only `succeeded` is tracked in [task discovery](task-discovery.spec.md)\n  §3.4 / §4 (`enforced: false`); this spec does not invent that policy.\n\n# 5. Constraints\n\n- One write transaction per change, using `tx.now`; the cycle check reads the\n  dependency graph inside it, so a concurrent edit cannot slip a cycle in.\n- No new table: `task_deps` already holds the graph.\n- Same authorization on REST and MCP; workers may not edit dependencies.\n\n# 6. Implementation Consequences\n\nOne PR:\n\n1. This spec, its index link, and worker lifecycle 3.2 and 3.6 pointing to it.\n2. `Action.DEPS_ADDED` (`ready → pending`) in the state machine.\n3. The endpoint, the MCP tool, the cycle check, and the inbox rows.\n4. Tests: replace and state changes in each allowed state, refusal in the\n   others, stale version, unknown ids, self and transitive cycles, the cancelled\n   dependency released by removal, inbox rows, totals and the `pending` filter,\n   and MCP forwarding.\n{\"type\":\"system\",\"subtype\":\"hook_started\",\"hook_id\":\"def9107f-4707-48f3-8bb6-551f7eecbe4b\",\"hook_name\":\"SessionStart:startup\",\"hook_event\":\"SessionStart\",\"uuid\":\"490f41ba-e80b-4a55-a0d2-953f1878a9a3\",\"session_id\":\"e60805ed-c36d-4e22-8274-acc0a44d64d1\"}\n{\"type\":\"system\",\"subtype\":\"hook_response\",\"hook_id\":\"def9107f-4707-48f3-8bb6-551f7eecbe4b\",\"hook_name\":\"SessionStart:startup\",\"hook_event\":\"SessionStart\",\"output\":\"## Memory\\n\\nThese files are loaded at startup, after clear, and after compaction:\\n\\n- `/workspace/agent/memory/index.md` - top-level memory index and Core Memory\\n- `/workspace/agent/memory/system/definition.md` - memory system behavior\\n\\nThe files on disk are authoritative. Edit them directly; follow links from\\nthe index when more detail is relevant.\\n\\n`memory/` is an Open Knowledge Format (OKF) v0.1 bundle: one Markdown\\nconcept per file, opened by a short YAML frontmatter with a `type`\\n(`index.md` and `log.md` are exempt; see the definition).\\n\\n### memory/index.md\\n\\n---\\nokf_version: \\\"0.1\\\"\\n---\\n\\n# Memory Index\\n\\n## Core Memory\\n\\nNothing stored yet. Keep this section to the few durable facts that matter in\\nnearly every conversation: who the user is, hard constraints, critical\\npreferences. Replace this note with your first core fact.\\n\\n## Map\\n\\n- [Memory system definition](system/definition.md) - how this memory works, and yours to improve\\n\\n### memory/system/definition.md\\n\\n---\\ntype: system\\n---\\n\\n# Agent Memory System\\n\\nThis file defines how your persistent memory works, and it is yours to improve.\\nOnly the portable file contract below and two loaded paths are fixed:\\n`memory/index.md` and this file at `memory/system/definition.md`. The folders,\\nprose organization, and other guidance are yours to reshape if a different\\nshape would remember or retrieve better.\\n\\n`memory/index.md` and this definition are loaded whenever a context window is\\ncreated: at startup, after clear, and after compaction. Keep both lean: headlines and\\npointers here, detail in linked files. Core Memory in the index should only hold\\ndurable facts relevant in nearly every conversation; behavior, role,\\nand persona belong in `/workspace/agent/instructions.prepend.md`.\\n\\n## Open Knowledge Format\\n\\nThe `memory/` directory follows the Open Knowledge Format (OKF): a simple\\nconvention for portable agent memory that any agent or tool can read and\\nedit. One Markdown concept per file, with YAML frontmatter containing a\\n`type`; `index.md` and `log.md` are reserved and do not need a type. The root\\n`index.md` declares `okf_version: \\\"0.1\\\"`.\\n\\nStart every new concept file like:\\n```yaml\\n---\\ntype: value\\n---\\n```\\n\\nOnly `type` is required: what kind of concept the file is. The optional OKF\\nfields:\\n\\n- `title` - display name\\n- `description` - one-line summary, used when scanning indexes and search hits\\n- `tags` - cross-cutting labels for search and grouping\\n- `resource` - path or URL of the raw source this was distilled from (e.g. a\\n ?? .argus-delegate/","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"e60805ed-c36d-4e22-8274-acc0a44d64d1","uuid":"5d5156e3-6600-4d03-9c8b-5057d0fe3ddc","timestamp":"2026-10-31T23:00:27.996Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"# Task edit\n\n# 1. Purpose and Scope\n\nAn operator or orchestrator who spots a wrong title, a thin spec, a missing\nacceptance criterion or a forgotten prerequisite can only cancel and recreate\nthe task today (only dependencies are editable, [task\ndependencies](task-dependencies.spec.md) 3.1). This spec adds **editing an\nexisting task's definition** with the same behaviour on every surface:\n\n- REST `PATCH /api/tasks/{id}`;\n- MCP `edit_task`;\n- the dashboard's Edit action on the task detail.\n\nAll three call one function, `tasks.service.edit_task`, which owns every rule\nbelow. `PUT /api/tasks/{id}/dependencies` and `set_task_dependencies` stay, as\nthin callers of the same function with only `depends_on` set.\n\n# 2. Non-Goals\n\n- No change to creation, retry, cancel, approval, archive or PR management.\n- No editing of a running attempt: an edit never reaches a worker that already\n  holds the task (3.4).\n- No edit history browser in the UI beyond the existing History list; the audit\n  is the `task.edited` event (3.7).\n- No per-project permissions (none exist, [knowledge](knowledge.spec.md) 3.1).\n\n# 3. Decisions Made\n\n## 3.1 Fields\n\n**Editable** (the only keys `changes` accepts):\n\n| Field | Type | Rules (same as `TaskCreate`) |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `title` | string | 1–200 characters; cannot be cleared |\n| `spec` | string | any length; `null` or `\"\"` clears it to `\"\"` |\n| `acceptance` | list of strings | `null` or `[]` clears |\n| `priority` | integer | cannot be cleared (send `0` to reset) |\n| `required_capabilities` | list of strings | de-duplicated and sorted, as at creation; `null` or `[]` clears |\n| `knowledge` | list of page ids | replaces the pinned set; every page must exist and belong to the task's project or to `global` (`422 knowledge: …`); `null` or `[]` unpins all. Pins a *kind* adds at creation are not re-added |\n| `difficulty` | `trivial` \\| `simple` \\| `standard` \\| `hard` | cannot be cleared; sets `difficulty_source` to `declared` ([task difficulty](task-difficulty.spec.md)) |\n| `depends_on` | list of task ids | replaces the set; the rules of [task dependencies](task-dependencies.spec.md) 3.1: ids exist, no self-dependency, no cycle, at most 100 |\n| `account_providers` | list of providers | engine providers only, de-duplicated and sorted, as at creation ([local harness](local-harness.spec.md) 3); `null` or `[]` clears (no restriction) |\n| `harness_model`, `executor_model` | object | [task models](task-models.spec.md) 3.1; `null` clears |\n\n**Immutable**: `state`/`status`, `assignee_agent_id`, `lease_expires_at`,\n`lease_ttl_s`, `timeout_s`, `timeout_at`, `attempt`, `failures`,\n`max_attempts`, `result`, `error`, `block_reason`, `progress_*`, `input`,\n`review`, `requires_approval`, `created_*`, `updated_at`, `archived_at`, any\nevent or history, and the structural ones `project`, `parent_id`, `kind`\n(and `id`). Naming any of them (or any unknown key) in `changes` is\n`422 immutable_field`, nothing is written, and `details` lists the offending\nkeys. They are not options of the MCP tool or of the dashboard form; the\nschema documents only the editable fields. `block_reason` is read-only in the\ntask detail facts too: it is shown as the inbox blocked card's sentence (worker\nlifecycle 3.2) and never edited there. State changes go through their own\noperations (approve, cancel, retry, …).\n\n## 3.2 Patch semantics\n\n- `changes` is a partial patch: a key that is **omitted is preserved**.\n  An empty `changes` is `422 validation_failed` (`changes: nothing to change`).\n- `null` is the explicit **clear** for `spec`, `acceptance`,\n  `required_capabilities`, `knowledge` and `depends_on` (equivalent to `\"\"` or\n  `[]`). `null` for `title` or `priority` is `422`.\n- A list is replaced as a whole, never merged.\n- A patch whose values all equal the current ones is a no-op: nothing is\n  written, no event, the version does not change, the current task comes back.\n- Validation is **atomic**: the patch is checked as a whole inside the write\n  transaction, before the first write. One bad field rejects the edit and the\n  task is untouched.\n\n## 3.3 Version precondition\n\nThe request carries `expected_version`, the `version` the caller read\n([MCP operations](mcp-operations.spec.md) 3.1). A mismatch is\n`409 stale_version` with the current version in `details`; nothing changes. A\nsuccessful edit that changed something always yields a new, strictly greater\n`version`, even within the clock's resolution. The response is the task, with\nits new `version`, and `get_task`/`get_tasks` read back the same version and\nvalues immediately.\n\n## 3.4 What each state permits\n\nThe single source of truth is `argus_core.task_edit.EDITABLE_BY_STATE`. The API\nreturns the allowed set as `editable_fields` on every task; the dashboard form\nand the MCP tool description are driven by it.\n\n| State | Editable fields | Why |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `awaiting_approval`, `pending`, `ready` | all seven | nothing has run; the next claim sees the saved definition |\n| `blocked`, `claimed`, `in_review` | `priority` | queue order only; the title, spec, criteria, capabilities, pins or dependencies may already be in use by an attempt or a reviewer |\n| `succeeded`, `failed`, `cancelled` | none | the record of what ran stays as it was |\n| archived (any) | none | archived tasks are read-only |\n\nA change outside the state's set is `409 task_not_editable`; `details` lists\n`state: <state>`, `not editable: <fields>` and `editable: <fields>`. We block;\nthere is no \"edit now, acknowledge later\" path.\n\n**Edit versus claim.** Writes are serialized, so the two outcomes are the only\nones: an edit that commits first is seen by the claim (the worker gets the new\ndefinition); a claim that commits first changes the task's `version` and state,\nso the edit fails `409 stale_version` (or `task_not_editable` if it was\nprepared against the new version). An edit never changes a task after a worker\nholds it, and Argus never assumes it updates a running attempt's prompt: a\nclaimed task keeps working on the definition it was handed.\n\n## 3.5 Attempt snapshot\n\nAt every claim Argus stores the definition the worker is given in\n`task_attempts` (`task_id`, `attempt`, `agent_id`, `claimed_at`, `definition`:\nthe seven editable fields plus `kind`, `project`). The definition is never\nupdated and rows are never deleted; an operator release only sets the row's\n`outcome` (`released`) and `outcome_reason` (migration 0031,\n[worker lifecycle](worker-lifecycle.spec.md) 3.3); `attempt` restarts at 1 after a retry, so they are listed in claim order. `GET /api/tasks/{id}/attempts` lists them, oldest first, so the\ndefinition of any attempt can be told apart from a later edit.\n\n## 3.6 Dependent state\n\nAfter `depends_on` changes the task follows the existing logic in the same\ntransaction ([task dependencies](task-dependencies.spec.md) 3.1): a `ready`\ntask with an unfinished dependency goes to `pending`; a `pending` one whose\ndependencies all `succeeded` becomes `ready`; `awaiting_approval` keeps its\nstate. `task.dependencies_changed` and `task.dependency_failed` events are\nraised as before.\n\n## 3.7 Audit\n\nEvery edit that changes something records one `task.edited` event in the\ntransaction: actor (`admin:<label>` or `agent:<id>`), time, `from_version`,\n`to_version` and `changes`, a diff per changed field:\n\n- `title`, `priority`, `complexity`: `{from, to}`;\n- `required_capabilities`, `knowledge`, `depends_on`: `{added, removed}` ids;\n- `spec`, `acceptance`: only lengths/counts and a SHA-256 digest of the old and\n  new text. The text itself is never copied into the event log, which is\n  streamed and shown in History: whatever an operator pasted into a spec, a\n  secret included, does not spread. The text of what each attempt ran is in the\n  attempt snapshot (3.5).\n\n## 3.8 Permissions\n\nOrchestrator and admin tokens edit; worker tokens get `403 forbidden` on REST\nand on MCP (the tool is not listed to them). This is the same authorization as\n`set_task_dependencies`; nothing is widened. There are no per-project\npermissions: project and kind cannot change, a knowledge page of another\nproject cannot be pinned, and error messages never echo another project's page\ncontent. `403` is decided before the version, state or content is looked at.\n\n## 3.9 Errors\n\nDomain errors keep the shape `{detail: {code, message, details}}` on REST and\n`<status> <code>: <message> | <details>` as a tool error on MCP.\n\n| Status | `code` | When |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| 403 | `forbidden` | not an orchestrator/admin token |\n| 404 | `not_found` | task, dependency or pinned page does not exist |\n| 409 | `stale_version` | `expected_version` is not current; `details`: `[current version]` |\n| 409 | `task_not_editable` | a field is outside the state's set (3.4) |\n| 422 | `immutable_field` | an immutable or unknown key (3.1) |\n| 422 | `validation_failed` | a value breaks a rule; each `details` entry is `<field>: <why>` |\n| 422 | `dependency_cycle` | self or transitive dependency; `details`: the path |\n\n## 3.10 Dashboard\n\nThe task detail shows **Edit** when `editable_fields` is not empty. The form is\nprefilled from the task, fields outside `editable_fields` are disabled, and\nSave sends only the fields the operator changed with the task's `version`.\nPer-field errors (`details` entries `<field>: …`, `dependency_cycle` and\nmissing dependencies on *Depends on*) show beside their field. On\n`stale_version` the form stays open with the operator's draft untouched, says\nwho is ahead, and offers *Reload latest* (discard the draft) or *Keep editing\non the latest version* (re-base the draft on the current version). A re-based\ndraft that changes a field the new state does not allow shows that field's\nerror instead of dropping the change. Live\nrefreshes never overwrite a draft. Cancel closes without a request.\n\n# 4. Open Questions\n\n- Whether `claimed` tasks should accept a spec edit delivered to the running\n  worker (a clarification-like message). Not without a worker-side contract;\n  [task steering](task-steering.spec.md) proposes one, as a message rather than an\n  edit.\n- Whether kind pins should be re-merged on edit.\n\n# 5. Constraints\n\n- One write transaction per edit, `tx.now` for time, no network call inside it.\n- One service function for all surfaces; REST, MCP and UI hold no rules of their\n  own and the MCP tool forwards to REST like every other tool.\n- The only schema change is the additive `task_attempts` table (migration 0024).\n- No secrets in the audit (3.7).\n\n# 6. Implementation Consequences\n\nOne PR:\n\n1. This spec, its index link, and pointers in task dependencies,\n   MCP operations and worker lifecycle (3.6, 3.7).\n2. Migration `0024_task_attempts.sql`; `argus_core.task_edit` (fields, state\n   policy, patch model); `Task.version` and `Task.editable_fields`.\n3. `tasks.service.edit_task` (and `set_dependencies` delegating to it), the\n   claim snapshot, `PATCH /api/tasks/{id}`, `GET /api/tasks/{id}/attempts`.\n4. MCP `edit_task`, instructions and tool catalog; OpenAPI and the TypeScript\n   client regenerated.\n5. Dashboard form, helpers and tests.\n6. Tests: parity (REST, MCP over the protocol, readback), atomic validation,\n   dependent state, concurrency and the edit/claim race, per-state policy,\n   permissions and immutables on both surfaces, snapshots, audit, and\n   dashboard component tests. Browser screenshots under `docs/evidence/` need\n   Playwright, which this repo does not have; they are not part of this PR.\n# Task dependencies\n\n# 1. Purpose and Scope\n\nDependencies are the only control flow between tasks\n([worker lifecycle](worker-lifecycle.spec.md) 3.2): a task with unfinished\ndependencies waits in `pending` and becomes `ready` once all of them\n`succeeded`. This spec closes three gaps in that model:\n\n1. **Editing.** Dependencies are fixed at creation. An orchestrator cannot add a\n   prerequisite it forgot or drop one that no longer matters, although the\n   scope table (worker lifecycle 3.6) says orchestrators \"edit deps\".\n2. **Visibility.** A dependent of a `failed` or `cancelled` task stays `pending`\n   with only a `task.dependency_failed` event. The inbox does not show it, so\n   it waits unnoticed.\n3. **Stranding.** A `cancelled` dependency can never succeed and cannot be\n   removed, so its dependents can only be cancelled or recreated.\n\n# 2. Non-Goals\n\n- No cascading: Argus still never cancels or fails a dependent on its own.\n- No ordering by shared resource (one task at a time per repository, say).\n  Order between tasks is declared, never inferred. That is a separate spec.\n- No conditional dependencies (\"run if A failed\"). A dependency means\n  \"succeeded first\".\n- No editing once a task was claimed: from `claimed` on, its work may already\n  rely on what its dependencies produced.\n\n# 3. Decisions Made\n\n## 3.1 Replacing dependencies\n\nSince the [task edit](task-edit.spec.md) spec this endpoint and tool call the same\nfunction as `PATCH /api/tasks/{id}` with only `depends_on`; the rules below hold\nunchanged, including the `task_not_waiting` code.\n\n- `PUT /api/tasks/{id}/dependencies` with `{depends_on: [ids], expected_version}`\n  (orchestrator or admin) → MCP `set_task_dependencies`. It replaces the whole\n  set; the response is the task, and its `version` changes.\n- Allowed while the task is `pending`, `awaiting_approval` or `ready`; otherwise\n  `409 task_not_waiting`. `expected_version` is the task's `version` (its\n  `updated_at`, MCP operations 3.1); a mismatch is `409 stale_version` with the\n  current version in `details`.\n- Every id must exist (`404`), the task cannot depend on itself, and the new set\n  cannot close a cycle: if a new dependency already depends on the task,\n  directly or transitively, it is `422 dependency_cycle` with the path in\n  `details`. At most 100 dependencies.\n- The state follows the new set in the same transaction: a `ready` task with an\n  unfinished dependency goes back to `pending` (a new `deps_added` action, the\n  only way back from `ready`; nothing holds a lease in `ready`); a `pending` task\n  whose dependencies all `succeeded` becomes `ready` (`deps_satisfied`, as\n  today). `awaiting_approval` keeps its state; approval already checks the\n  dependencies.\n- It records `task.dependencies_changed` with `added` and `removed`. A newly\n  added dependency that is already `failed` or `cancelled` raises\n  `task.dependency_failed`, as at creation.\n- Removing a `cancelled` or `failed` dependency is how a stranded task is\n  released (gap 3): no other mechanism is added.\n- A read-only [prerequisite evidence](task-discovery.spec.md) view reports\n  whether each direct dependency is forge-merged or artifact-verified. It\n  changes nothing here: readiness still keys only on `succeeded`.\n\n## 3.2 Stuck dependents in the inbox\n\n- The inbox (MCP operations 3.2) also lists `pending` tasks with at least one\n  `failed` or `cancelled` dependency. Their row has no decision and carries\n  `failed_dependencies: [ids]`; every other row has an empty list. They wait\n  since the latest of those dependencies ended.\n- `states` accepts `pending` for these rows; they count under `pending` in\n  `totals_by_state` and under `none` in `totals_by_decision_kind`.\n- The orchestrator then retries the dependency (`retry_task`, if `failed`),\n  replaces the dependencies (3.1), or cancels the dependent.\n\n# 4. Open Questions\n\n- Whether a dependent's claim should carry its dependencies' result summaries,\n  so a worker does not have to read them with `get_tasks`. Decide once workers\n  ask for it.\n- Whether readiness should require merged/verified delivery evidence rather\n  than only `succeeded` is tracked in [task discovery](task-discovery.spec.md)\n  §3.4 / §4 (`enforced: false`); this spec does not invent that policy.\n\n# 5. Constraints\n\n- One write transaction per change, using `tx.now`; the cycle check reads the\n  dependency graph inside it, so a concurrent edit cannot slip a cycle in.\n- No new table: `task_deps` already holds the graph.\n- Same authorization on REST and MCP; workers may not edit dependencies.\n\n# 6. Implementation Consequences\n\nOne PR:\n\n1. This spec, its index link, and worker lifecycle 3.2 and 3.6 pointing to it.\n2. `Action.DEPS_ADDED` (`ready → pending`) in the state machine.\n3. The endpoint, the MCP tool, the cycle check, and the inbox rows.\n4. Tests: replace and state changes in each allowed state, refusal in the\n   others, stale version, unknown ids, self and transitive cycles, the cancelled\n   dependency released by removal, inbox rows, totals and the `pending` filter,\n   and MCP forwarding.\n{\"type\":\"system\",\"subtype\":\"hook_started\",\"hook_id\":\"def9107f-4707-48f3-8bb6-551f7eecbe4b\",\"hook_name\":\"SessionStart:startup\",\"hook_event\":\"SessionStart\",\"uuid\":\"490f41ba-e80b-4a55-a0d2-953f1878a9a3\",\"session_id\":\"e60805ed-c36d-4e22-8274-acc0a44d64d1\"}\n{\"type\":\"system\",\"subtype\":\"hook_response\",\"hook_id\":\"def9107f-4707-48f3-8bb6-551f7eecbe4b\",\"hook_name\":\"SessionStart:startup\",\"hook_event\":\"SessionStart\",\"output\":\"## Memory\\n\\nThese files are loaded at startup, after clear, and after compaction:\\n\\n- `/workspace/agent/memory/index.md` - top-level memory index and Core Memory\\n- `/workspace/agent/memory/system/definition.md` - memory system behavior\\n\\nThe files on disk are authoritative. Edit them directly; follow links from\\nthe index when more detail is relevant.\\n\\n`memory/` is an Open Knowledge Format (OKF) v0.1 bundle: one Markdown\\nconcept per file, opened by a short YAML frontmatter with a `type`\\n(`index.md` and `log.md` are exempt; see the definition).\\n\\n### memory/index.md\\n\\n---\\nokf_version: \\\"0.1\\\"\\n---\\n\\n# Memory Index\\n\\n## Core Memory\\n\\nNothing stored yet. Keep this section to the few durable facts that matter in\\nnearly every conversation: who the user is, hard constraints, critical\\npreferences. Replace this note with your first core fact.\\n\\n## Map\\n\\n- [Memory system definition](system/definition.md) - how this memory works, and yours to improve\\n\\n### memory/system/definition.md\\n\\n---\\ntype: system\\n---\\n\\n# Agent Memory System\\n\\nThis file defines how your persistent memory works, and it is yours to improve.\\nOnly the portable file contract below and two loaded paths are fixed:\\n`memory/index.md` and this file at `memory/system/definition.md`. The folders,\\nprose organization, and other guidance are yours to reshape if a different\\nshape would remember or retrieve better.\\n\\n`memory/index.md` and this definition are loaded whenever a context window is\\ncreated: at startup, after clear, and after compaction. Keep both lean: headlines and\\npointers here, detail in linked files. Core Memory in the index should only hold\\ndurable facts relevant in nearly every conversation; behavior, role,\\nand persona belong in `/workspace/agent/instructions.prepend.md`.\\n\\n## Open Knowledge Format\\n\\nThe `memory/` directory follows the Open Knowledge Format (OKF): a simple\\nconvention for portable agent memory that any agent or tool can read and\\nedit. One Markdown concept per file, with YAML frontmatter containing a\\n`type`; `index.md` and `log.md` are reserved and do not need a type. The root\\n`index.md` declares `okf_version: \\\"0.1\\\"`.\\n\\nStart every new concept file like:\\n```yaml\\n---\\ntype: value\\n---\\n```\\n\\nOnly `type` is required: what kind of concept the file is. The optional OKF\\nfields:\\n\\n- `title` - display name\\n- `description` - one-line summary, used when scanning indexes and search hits\\n- `tags` - cross-cutting labels for search and grouping\\n- `resource` - path or URL of the raw source this was distilled from (e.g. a\\n ?? .argus-delegate/","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01VJpaKbEs68ynJMzo76jmfM","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"rule"}}]}
