{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgb9T7MNPkz8LLck8pNox","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01QooW3h3apwezsssnyv6KE6","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"grep -n \"edit_task\\|set_task_dependencies\\|create_task\\|WORKER_TOOLS\\|_MANAGER\\|def _call\\|def _post\\|def _patch\\|def _put\\|async def _req\\|tool_catalog\\|TOOLS\" apps/api/src/argus_api/mcp_server.py | head -60; sed -n 1,140p apps/api/src/argus_api/mcp_server.py"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2204,"cache_read_input_tokens":57834,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2204},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"e60805ed-c36d-4e22-8274-acc0a44d64d1","uuid":"83ee20f1-a328-47b3-87ad-4489cd11d194","timestamp":"2026-10-31T23:01:00.437Z","request_id":"req_011Cgb9T76zAEQTkxMAP5qzY","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01QooW3h3apwezsssnyv6KE6":{"command":"cd /workspace/agent/task-workspaces/030540ef-d69a-4c2c-839d-f4970e8c94d7 && grep -n \"edit_task\\|set_task_dependencies\\|create_task\\|WORKER_TOOLS\\|_MANAGER\\|def _call\\|def _post\\|def _patch\\|def _put\\|async def _req\\|tool_catalog\\|TOOLS\" apps/api/src/argus_api/mcp_server.py | head -60; sed -n 1,140p apps/api/src/argus_api/mcp_server.py"}},"wire_ingest_context":{"toolu_01QooW3h3apwezsssnyv6KE6":{"cwd":"/workspace/agent/task-workspaces/030540ef-d69a-4c2c-839d-f4970e8c94d7"}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01QooW3h3apwezsssnyv6KE6","type":"tool_result","content":"62:request_id, so retries are safe); get_tasks(ids) reads the effects back. edit_task changes a task's title, spec,\n261:async def _call(\n745:async def create_task(\n1105:    retry it or set_task_dependencies), plus open pool and knowledge decisions. Each row\n1171:async def edit_task(\n1199:async def set_task_dependencies(\n\"\"\"MCP endpoint for agents (worker lifecycle 3.7), served at /mcp/.\n\nEvery tool is a thin call to the REST API, made in-process with the agent's own\nbearer token. Scopes, validation, leases, and errors therefore behave exactly\nas over HTTP; this module holds no state or logic of its own.\n\nConnect with `Authorization: Bearer <agent token>` (or an admin token for the\norchestrator-style tools).\n\"\"\"\n\nfrom base64 import b64decode\nfrom collections.abc import AsyncGenerator, Callable\nfrom contextlib import asynccontextmanager\nfrom datetime import datetime\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfrom types import FunctionType\nfrom typing import Annotated, Any, Literal\nfrom urllib.parse import urlsplit\n\nimport httpx2 as httpx\nfrom argus_core.auth import Scope\nfrom argus_core.continuation import (\n    ChecklistReport,\n    ContinuationUpdate,\n    ObservationRequest,\n)\nfrom argus_core.task_edit import TaskChanges\nfrom argus_core.tasks import ExecutorModel, HarnessModel\nfrom fastapi import FastAPI\nfrom mcp.server.context import CallNext, HandlerResult, ServerRequestContext\nfrom mcp.server.mcpserver import Context, MCPServer\nfrom mcp.server.mcpserver.exceptions import ToolError\nfrom mcp.server.mcpserver.resources import TextResource\nfrom mcp.server.transport_security import TransportSecuritySettings\nfrom mcp.shared.exceptions import MCPError\nfrom mcp.types import INVALID_REQUEST\nfrom pydantic import BaseModel, Field\nfrom starlette.types import ASGIApp, Receive, Send\nfrom starlette.types import Scope as AsgiScope\n\nfrom argus_api.auth import authenticate\nfrom argus_api.errors import UnauthorizedError\n\nINSTRUCTIONS = \"\"\"\\\nArgus coordinates a fleet of agents. As a worker: claim_task, then work on it while\ncalling heartbeat (renews your leases) and report_progress; finish with complete_task\nor fail_task. Every task write needs the task's `attempt`; a 409 error means you no\nlonger hold the task, so stop working on it. If you need a human or orchestrator to\ndecide something, use ask_question (your task is paused until answered). Read the\nknowledge pages listed with each claimed task before starting; search_knowledge for\nmore, and write_knowledge when you learn something others will need. Lease provider\ncredentials only after claiming a task, with runtime_providers, task_id and attempt.\nIf an account runs out of quota, report_account_exhausted (same claim and providers)\nreturns fallback metadata; call lease_account to receive the next credential.\n\nAs an orchestrator, work the inbox: get_inbox lists what waits (blocked, in review,\nawaiting approval) with decision and artifact ids; list_decisions(view=\"detail\") or\nget_decision reads the questions (get_inbox(view=\"actionable\") gives, per row,\nwho may answer, what each answer does, what it holds up and labelled evidence: hints\nonly, nothing is executed); list_artifacts, verify_artifact and read_artifact\ncheck evidence; answer_decision or answer_decisions acts (give each answer a\nrequest_id, so retries are safe); get_tasks(ids) reads the effects back. edit_task changes a task's title, spec,\nacceptance, priority, capabilities, pins or dependencies (pass its `version`). Lists\nare paged: `total` counts every match, follow `next_cursor` while `has_more`. If a\ntool you need is missing, say which and stop; do not script REST around it.\n\nFor the fleet: inspect_pool shows what each instance is proven to run (a fact's\n`source`, or `unknown`); recycle_instance and drain_agent never interrupt claimed\nwork (recycle_instance(requeue=true) and release_task requeue it without costing an\nattempt); admins change options with update_pool(expected_version=the pool's version).\nget_agent_hygiene reports lost/gone aggregates and alerts; archive_agents (admin)\narchives old lost/gone identities in bounded batches. wait_for_events(subject_type,\nsubject_id) waits for the change to happen instead of polling; then inspect_pool or\nget_tasks reads back the effect. steer_task sends an instruction to the worker\nholding a claimed task; it arrives on that worker's next heartbeat.\n\nOrchestrators: read the skill at skill://argus-orchestrator/SKILL.md (also the\nprompt \"argus-orchestrator\") before acting: what you must and must not do.\"\"\"\n\nserver = MCPServer(\"argus\", version=\"0.1.0\", instructions=INSTRUCTIONS)\n\n# The orchestrator skill, in the Agent Skills format (SKILL.md), served as an MCP\n# resource and as a prompt so any connected client can load it (MCP operations 3.4).\nORCHESTRATOR_SKILL = (\n    Path(__file__).parent / \"skills\" / \"argus-orchestrator\" / \"SKILL.md\"\n).read_text()\nserver.add_resource(\n    TextResource(\n        uri=\"skill://argus-orchestrator/SKILL.md\",\n        name=\"argus-orchestrator\",\n        title=\"Argus orchestrator skill\",\n        description=\"What an orchestrator can and must do with the Argus tools.\",\n        mime_type=\"text/markdown\",\n        text=ORCHESTRATOR_SKILL,\n    )\n)\n\n\n@server.prompt(\n    name=\"argus-orchestrator\",\n    title=\"Argus orchestrator\",\n    description=\"Load the Argus orchestrator skill before running the fleet.\",\n)\ndef orchestrator_skill() -> str:\n    return ORCHESTRATOR_SKILL\n\n\n_app: FastAPI | None = None\n\n# Who a tool is listed to (MCP operations 3.4). Listing is not access control:\n# every call is still decided by the REST endpoint it forwards to.\nAGENTS = frozenset({Scope.WORKER, Scope.ORCHESTRATOR})\nMANAGERS = frozenset({Scope.ORCHESTRATOR, Scope.ADMIN})\nADMINS = frozenset({Scope.ADMIN})\nANYONE = frozenset(Scope)\naudiences: dict[str, frozenset[Scope]] = {}\n\n\ndef tool[F: FunctionType](audience: frozenset[Scope]) -> Callable[[F], F]:\n    def register(fn: F) -> F:\n        audiences[fn.__name__] = audience\n        return server.tool()(fn)\n\n    return register\n\n\nasync def _list_tools_for_caller(\n    ctx: ServerRequestContext[Any, Any], call_next: CallNext\n) -> HandlerResult:\n    \"\"\"Answer `tools/list` with only the tools the caller's scope can use.\"\"\"\n    result = await call_next(ctx)\n    # The SDK hands middleware the serialized result; tests pin that shape.\n    if ctx.method != \"tools/list\" or not isinstance(result, dict):\n        return result\n    headers = getattr(ctx.request, \"headers\", None) or {}\n    scheme, _, token = headers.get(\"authorization\", \"\").partition(\" \")\n    assert _app is not None\n    try:\n        if scheme.lower() != \"bearer\" or not token:\n            raise UnauthorizedError(\"missing bearer token\")","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"e60805ed-c36d-4e22-8274-acc0a44d64d1","uuid":"9126a28c-cc66-48cf-a65b-062c51b69fa8","timestamp":"2026-10-31T23:01:00.477Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"62:request_id, so retries are safe); get_tasks(ids) reads the effects back. edit_task changes a task's title, spec,\n261:async def _call(\n745:async def create_task(\n1105:    retry it or set_task_dependencies), plus open pool and knowledge decisions. Each row\n1171:async def edit_task(\n1199:async def set_task_dependencies(\n\"\"\"MCP endpoint for agents (worker lifecycle 3.7), served at /mcp/.\n\nEvery tool is a thin call to the REST API, made in-process with the agent's own\nbearer token. Scopes, validation, leases, and errors therefore behave exactly\nas over HTTP; this module holds no state or logic of its own.\n\nConnect with `Authorization: Bearer <agent token>` (or an admin token for the\norchestrator-style tools).\n\"\"\"\n\nfrom base64 import b64de